# st.stm32l476.freertos_demo
# Real STM32CubeMX-generated (STM32L476RGTx, Makefile toolchain, CMSIS_V1
# FreeRTOS) two-task demo - proves genuine FreeRTOS task-switch telemetry
# end to end (RescParser's pxCurrentTCB/vTaskSwitchContext detection ->
# UnicornCore.on_task_switch -> session.py -> Timeline's RTOS pane), not
# just that a bare-metal loop runs. Also carries uart_gpio_demo's full
# bare-metal peripheral set (ADC1/DAC1/DMA/I2C1/SPI2), exercised once in
# main() before osKernelStart() - see README.md's "FreeRTOS demo gets
# uart_gpio_demo's peripherals too" section for why there, not in a task.
# See ../../../../../replica-server/renoly_examples/st/stm32l476/README.md.

using sysbus
mach create "STM32L476_FreeRTOS_Demo"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platform.repl

# Load firmware first so symbols resolve for patching. Bare @$ORIGIN/
# firmware.elf, not the Makefile build output path directly - see
# uart_gpio_demo/sim.resc's own doc comment on this exact same fix (this
# demo copied the same now-corrected mistake): server/bundle.py's
# materialize_bundle only literal-string-replaces "$ORIGIN/firmware.elf",
# so anything else silently fails to load over the real client->server
# path (StartSession/RunSimulation) even though it resolves fine over a
# direct RescParser.parse() (pytest/CLI tooling) - which is exactly why
# this demo's Timeline stayed empty in the real extension despite the
# task-switch/interrupt-entry wiring being genuinely correct. A synced
# flat firmware.elf copy lives at this demo's own root (sibling to this
# file) - see README.md and the pytest/CLI tooling's auto-sync step.
sysbus LoadELF @$ORIGIN/firmware.elf

# Same established HAL_InitTick patch as uart_gpio_demo's own sim.resc:
# the emulator's MachineTimer drives SysTick delivery itself, independent
# of the real peripheral's HAL-configured reload value.
python "monitor.Parse('sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x%X 0x47702000' % (monitor.Machine.SystemBus.GetSymbolAddress('HAL_InitTick') & ~1))"
# HAL_Delay patch, defensive - this demo's own tasks use osDelay() (FreeRTOS's
# tick count, not HAL_GetTick()), but the newly-added ADC1/DAC1/I2C1/SPI2 HAL
# code (transplanted from uart_gpio_demo, which DOES call HAL_Delay from its
# own main loop) may or may not pull a real HAL_Delay reference into this
# build depending on what the linker keeps - patching it if present costs
# nothing; RescParser logs a harmless, noisy-but-safe error instead of a
# silent no-op if it turns out unreferenced here.
python "monitor.Parse('sysbus WriteWord 0x%X 0x4770' % (monitor.Machine.SystemBus.GetSymbolAddress('HAL_Delay') & ~1))"

# USART2_TDR write watchpoint - observes uartTask's marker/heartbeat bytes
# as they're transmitted (real, timing-independent proof it actually ran,
# not poked - same idiom as uart_gpio_demo's own USART2 watchpoint).
sysbus AddWatchpointHook 0x40004428 4 Write "print(chr(value & 0xFF), end='')"

# Peripheral watchpoints below use the same "[Tag] Description" convention
# every other example project's sim.resc already uses (CAN_demo_2's own
# I2C1/SPI1 hooks, pwm_demo's SPI5/TIM hooks, etc.) - server/session.py's
# WatchpointHook.label/description pulls the tag straight out of a
# bracketed prefix in the print text, and is_byte_tap (see its own
# _BYTE_TAP_MARKERS) decides whether the hook lands as a Timeline Event
# or a Bus Traffic frame. This untags a real gap the earlier version of
# this file had: the I2C1 hook printed a bare, unbracketed string - still
# a genuine Event (is_byte_tap is false for it), but labeled with the raw
# address/"register write" instead of reading as I2C1 at a glance - and
# ADC1/DAC1/SPI2 had no watchpoints at all, so their only visible trace
# was the UART marker bytes on the Bus Traffic pane, not a distinct
# signal of their own.

# I2C1 CR2 (0x40005404) write watchpoint, firing only when the real
# I2C_GENERATE_START() macro's START bit (bit13=0x2000) is set. Not a
# byte-tap -> lands as an [I2C1] Event.
sysbus AddWatchpointHook 0x40005404 4 Write "if value & 0x2000: print('[I2C1] CR2 START condition')"

# ADC1 CR (0x50040008) write watchpoint, firing only when the real
# LL_ADC_REG_StartConversion() bit (ADSTART, bit2=0x4) is set - direct
# proof HAL_ADC_Start() genuinely issued a start, not just configured one.
# Not a byte-tap -> lands as an [ADC1] Event.
sysbus AddWatchpointHook 0x50040008 4 Write "if value & 0x4: print('[ADC1] CR ADSTART set - regular conversion started')"

# DAC1 DHR12R1 (0x40007408) write watchpoint - the real 12-bit right-
# aligned code HAL_DAC_SetValue() writes. %03X (3 hex digits, a 12-bit
# value) deliberately avoids is_byte_tap's %02X/X2/%x markers (those are
# for single-byte UART/SPI/I2C taps) -> lands as a [DAC1] Event, not a
# byte-stream bus frame.
sysbus AddWatchpointHook 0x40007408 4 Write "print('[DAC1] DHR12R1=0x%03X' % (value & 0xFFF))"

# SPI2 DR (0x4000380C) write watchpoint - same real idiom as every SPI-
# touching example in this repo (CAN_demo_2's [SPI1 TX], pwm_demo's
# [SPI5 TX]): the exact byte HAL_SPI_TransmitReceive() clocks out. IS a
# byte-tap (%02X matches is_byte_tap's own markers, by design - SPI is a
# byte-stream peripheral like UART) -> lands as Bus Traffic on an "SPI2
# TX" bus lane, not a Timeline Event, matching every other SPI example.
sysbus AddWatchpointHook 0x4000380C 1 Write "print('[SPI2 TX] DR=0x%02X' % (value & 0xFF))"

start
